      "content": "The shift to a decentralized network will inevitably introduce more variables into our supply chain, potentially increasing the risk of delays in life-saving drug delivery due to miscommunication or resource misallocation across the new network nodes. In a worst-case scenario, such as during a regional supply chain disruption, we face potential delays that could range from 1-3 business days for a localized area, stemming from slower capacity scans and higher incident response times compared to our centralized control. Moreover, we're looking at an anticipated 15% contingency cost to uphold a 'door-to-door' delivery promise, factoring for additional reserves we must maintain at each regional node. While difficult to pinpoint exact risk zones without more granular analysis, historical data shows that tropical regions with higher infrastructure risks could see upwards of a 20-30% chance of supply chain disruption per month, compared to a 5-10% expectation with centralized control. The higher complexity could cumulatively translate into reflecting a 10-25% potential decrease in on-time delivery performance for the most at-risk drugs during peak consumption seasons, such as flu or organ transplants, which our current centralized system has efficiently managed year over year.",
